g or national environment shifts toward either party could move this several points.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island remains a Democratic-leaning state in presidential elections, with a partisan lean of about eight points toward Democrats. The incumbent president is term-limited, making this an open contest with no incumbent advantage on either side. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market pricing are available for this race, so the outlook rests almost entirely on the state's underlying partisan lean.\n\nThe forecast expects a Democratic win of roughly eight points, consistent with the state's fundamentals. Uncertainty is elevated because direct evidence is thin; a strong national environment for either party could move the margin meaningfully. Rhode Island's Democratic governor losing a primary over a bridge controversy shows local volatility, though its relevance to presidential voting is unclear.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8249, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7734, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7988, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7688, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8114, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8082, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7696, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.7776, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.7932, "margin": 8.0}]}